# the argument of locke-treatise-4.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Locke, Second Treatise sect. 171: political power is the natural power handed to society in trust, so its end is preservation and it cannot be absolute
atom	pp_given_up	political power is that power which every man has in the state of nature and has given up to the society and its governors, with trust that it be employed for their good and the preservation of their property
atom	nat_power_preserve	the power every man has in the state of nature is to use such means as he thinks good and nature allows to preserve his property, and to punish breaches of the law of nature so as may most conduce to the preservation of himself and mankind
atom	end_nat_preservation	the end and measure of this power in every man's hands in the state of nature is the preservation of all mankind
atom	end_mag_preserve	in the hands of the magistrate the power can have no other end or measure but to preserve the members of the society in their lives, liberties and possessions
atom	not_absolute	the power cannot be an absolute, arbitrary power over the lives and fortunes of the members
atom	power_laws	the power is a power to make laws and annex penalties tending to the preservation of the whole, cutting off only those parts so corrupt that they threaten the sound
atom	compact_origin	this power has its original only from compact and agreement and the mutual consent of those who make up the community
P1	S3	pp_given_up	kind conceptual
P2	S3	nat_power_preserve	kind conceptual
C1	S4	end_nat_preservation	from P2
A1	-	pp_given_up ∧ end_nat_preservation → end_mag_preserve
C2	S4	end_mag_preserve	from P1 C1 A1
P3	S4	end_mag_preserve → not_absolute
P4	S4	end_mag_preserve → power_laws
C	S4	not_absolute ∧ power_laws	from C2 P3 P4
# S5 is stated but not used in deriving the conclusion
P5	S5	compact_origin
